# The preview pane: every body it can show, and the two rules that make it worth having.
#
# Nothing here presses play on a media file. The pane stops at the poster frame until someone
# does, which is what keeps this run silent and keeps every capture a real picture — a MediaElement
# renders through its own composition surface and comes back as a hole in a RenderTargetBitmap.

tree .
preview-fixture
refresh
go Preview
assert-count 8

# --- off by default, and toggling is per tab ---
#
# The caption strip's toggle is the discoverable way in — Ctrl+P and the Settings checkbox are
# the other two, and the checkbox only says what a *new* tab starts from. It is asserted here
# rather than photographed: whether it is lit is a title-bar detail the window shots below carry.

assert-visible PreviewPaneToggle
assert-hidden PreviewPane
preview on
assert-visible PreviewPane
shot 01-empty PreviewPane

# --- an image, with its dimensions and the chequerboard behind its transparency ---

select sample.png
settle 400
assert-preview image
shot 02-image PreviewPane

# --- code, coloured and selectable ---

select Sample.cs
settle 400
assert-preview text
shot 03-code PreviewPane

# --- markdown, which Explorer's pane shows nothing at all for ---

select sample.md
settle 400
assert-preview text
shot 04-markdown PreviewPane

# --- an archive, listed without extracting anything ---

select sample.zip
settle 400
assert-preview archive
shot 05-archive PreviewPane

# --- plain text, so the encoding and line-ending footer is on screen ---

select plain.txt
settle 400
assert-preview text
shot 06-text PreviewPane

# --- the shell has no preview for these, so the bytes get a look before giving up ---
#
# A .manifest is XML that Explorer's pane refuses outright. The .qqq beside it is an extension no
# table will ever carry, and is the real test: a list of extensions has an endless tail, and the
# content sniff is what covers it.

select app.exe.manifest
settle 400
assert-preview text
shot 11-manifest PreviewPane

select mystery.qqq
settle 400
assert-preview text

# ...and the other half of the rule: something genuinely binary is still refused rather than shown
# as a screen of mojibake. Mutate IsConvincingText to return true and this line goes red.
select opaque.qqq
settle 400
assert-preview none

# --- hex and raw: the two things the refusal above is now allowed to be an invitation to ---
#
# The dead-end is where hex is offered, not taken — nothing starts dumping bytes at someone
# arrowing down a folder of executables. Switching is what asks for it.

preview-mode hex
assert-preview hex
shot 12-hex PreviewPane

# Raw over a PNG is the clearest picture of what forcing text is for: the header reads, the rest
# is Latin-1 noise, and nothing was refused. It is also the case that proves the override beats
# the extension table rather than sitting behind it.

select sample.png
preview-mode raw
assert-preview text
shot 13-raw PreviewPane

# Sticky across selections, the way Lister's is — and reversible. Put the mode reset back into
# Show() and the assertion above this one goes red.

preview-mode auto
assert-preview image

# --- a folder and a multi-selection both say what they are rather than going blank ---

select-all
settle 400
assert-preview none
shot 07-many PreviewPane

deselect
settle 400
assert-preview none
state

# --- the whole window, so the splitter and the list beside it are in one picture ---

select sample.png
settle 400
shot 08-window

# --- the invariant that justifies the whole feature: previewing holds nothing open ---
#
# Explorer's pane locks the file it is showing; this one reads into memory and closes. If that
# ever regresses, the rename below fails and this script goes red rather than the bug reaching
# someone mid-move.

select Sample.cs
settle 400
assert-preview text
rename Renamed.cs
assert-row Renamed.cs
select Renamed.cs
delete
assert-missing Renamed.cs
undo
assert-row Renamed.cs

# --- and it survives a theme change, since the syntax colours are tokens like everything else ---

select Renamed.cs
settle 400
theme light-plus
shot 09-code-light PreviewPane

# The dump borrows the same five syntax colours, so this is where a palette that reads on Dark+
# and vanishes two shades lighter would show up.
preview-mode hex
shot 14-hex-light PreviewPane
preview-mode auto

theme dark-plus

# --- off again, and the list gets its width back ---

preview off
assert-hidden PreviewPane
shot 10-off
